<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Farrukhan the Great</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 712–728</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Farrukhan the Great was the ispahbad of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a>, the mountainous Caspian province of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, and the most powerful ruler of the Dabuyid dynasty. In 717 he defeated the invading army of the Umayyad general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazid-ibn-al-muhallab">Yazid ibn al-Muhallab</a> in the forests of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alborz">Alborz</a>, forcing a treaty in which he paid only a modest tribute. His victory preserved the Dabuyids' independence for another generation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/farrukhan-the-great.jpg" alt="Farrukhan the Great" width="320" height="310" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Classical Numismatic Group; [1] · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_Farrukhan_the_Great_(1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Little is written about the Dabuyids. They are known mainly from the local histories of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-isfandiyar">Ibn Isfandiyar</a> (early 13th century) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zahir-al-din-marashi">Zahir al-Din Mar'ashi</a> (died after 1489), with brief mentions by al-Tabari, Ibn al-Athir and the geographers Ibn al-Faqih and Ibn Khurdadbih. Both local historians trace the family to the Sasanian royal house through Jamasp. Their rule over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilan">Gilan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daylam">Daylam</a> and the mountains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a> was nominal: local chiefs and the Qarinvandid and Bavandid dynasties held real power there, and the Dabuyids ruled directly only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruyan">Ruyan</a> and the valleys of Tabaristan as far as Tammisha.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Farrukhan bore the titles ispahbadh, Padashwargarshah and Gilgilan. He first agreed to pay tribute to the Turks to stop their raids, then fortified the passes into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a> and stopped paying after two years; when they invaded, he defeated them in a night attack. A revolt of his Daylamite subjects drove him to the castle of Firuz-Khusra at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amol">Amul</a>, and the besiegers withdrew when he had clay shaped like loaves of bread set on the walls to look well provisioned.</p><p>When the Arab governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and Khurasan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazid-ibn-al-muhallab">Yazid ibn al-Muhallab</a>, invaded, he at first defeated Farrukhan and took Dihistan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorgan">Gurgan</a>. Farrukhan then raised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daylamites">Daylamites</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilaks">Gilites</a>, defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> and stirred Gurgan to revolt; its Arab garrison was massacred, including fifty members of Yazid's family. After the treaty that followed, the caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umar-ibn-abd-al-aziz">Umar II</a> had Yazid dismissed and imprisoned.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-isfandiyar">Ibn Isfandiyar</a> tells that Farrukhan ordered a man named Bav to build a city at a place called Ohar, but Bav was bribed to build it elsewhere and was hanged; the city, Sari, took the name of Farrukhan's son Saruya. Beside it Farrukhan built a palace called Shahr-e Espahbodan. He is the first Dabuyid whose coins are known, half-weight imitations of those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-ii">Khosrow II</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Middle Persian</a> legends and a winged crown referring to Verethragna, the god of victory, a pattern his successors and the later Abbasid governors kept. He died in 728 and was succeeded by his son Dadhburzmihr.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Farrukhan the Great” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Farrukhan the Great</b> defeated the Arab general Yazid ibn al-Muhallab in 717, and the dynasty paid tribute only when forced.</blockquote><a href="/state/dabuyid-dynasty">Dabuyid dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sari-iran">Sari</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.57,53.06&amp;year=720" aria-label="Sari on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5883 845 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Farrukhan the Great"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6333 1115h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6333 1115h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=36.57,53.06&amp;year=720">What was here in 720 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
